        "O2",
        "O7",
        "M2",
        "M3",
        "M4"
      ],
      "text": "N1 is already recognized despite its pending display. Higher-dose bulk predicts its duplicate at tick 1, shifting N4 recognition to tick 4. The tick-4 bulk consequently queues N4 again; that duplicate consumes tick 5 and pushes N8 beyond the window. These are finite-model deductions, not observed trial results."
    },
    {
      "id": "r08_output_guard",
      "source_refs": [
        "C1",
        "C2",
        "C3",
        "C5",
        "O1",
        "O2",
        "O5",
        "P1",
        "P2",
        "M1",
        "M2"
      ],
      "text": "Track registrations separately from distinct sender recognition. Six registrations and seven recognitions remain initially. Requests, failures and duplicates cannot count as recognition. Empty initial queue prevents a tick-0 delivery; next-boundary eligibility makes tick-8 requests too late."
    },
    {
      "id": "r08_burden_unknowns",
      "source_refs": [
        "C3",
        "C6",
        "R1",
        "R2",
        "R6",
        "U1",
        "U2",
        "U3"
      ],
      "text": "Each declared schedule requires six one-tick validations and six dispatcher actions; reading ledgers adds no clerk labor. Exceptional display-delay origin, later sender adaptation and empirical generalization remain unknown. No new demand occurs presently. No measured threshold, successful execution or global-optimality proof is claimed."
    }
  ],
  "summary": [
    {
      "id": "r08_fixed_comparison",
      "source_refs": [
        "C1",
        "C2",
        "C3",
        "C4",
        "C5",
        "O1",
        "O2",
        "O3",
        "O4",
        "O6",
        "O7",
        "R1",
        "R2",
        "R3",
        "R4"
      ],
      "text": "R08 fixes eight registrations and eight distinct recognized receipts by boundary 9 within six dispatcher actions. Register N3,N4,N5,N6,N7,N8 at ticks 0,1,2,3,4,5. Concrete normal schedule: individual N2 at 0, N3 at 1, N4 at 2, bulk at 4, individual N7 at 5 and N8 at 6. Closest rival replaces only tick-0 individual with bulk. Bulk dose rises from one to two; both complete eight registrations and spend six actions. Exact comparison metric: distinct recognitions by boundary 9."
    },
    {
      "id": "r08_trigger_probe",
      "source_refs": [
        "C1",
        "C6",
        "M1",
        "M2",
        "M3",
        "M4",
        "R6",
        "R8",
        "U3"
      ],
      "text": "Predeclared separating probe: two independently reset matched trial pairs with identical initial demand, states and timings. Switch only if BOTH higher-dose trials have fewer distinct recognitions than their normal controls; otherwise hold. Inspect registration, recognition, action and queue ledgers. Reset and four-trial resources are unsupplied, so execution remains an untestable assumption."
    },
    {
      "id": "r08_no_opening",
      "source_refs": [
        "C1",
        "C3",
        "C4",
        "O2",
        "R2",
        "R4",
        "R8"
      ],
      "text": "No opening: depriving the bulk tool leaves individual requests only. Seven unrecognized identities require seven actions, exceeding six; recognition ceiling is seven including N1. This available route cannot preserve the fixed success function. Do not activate that exit even if the harmful-dose trigger were confirmed."
    },
    {
      "id": "r08_predictions_defeat",
      "source_refs": [
        "C2",
        "C4",
        "C5",
        "O2",
        "O4",
        "O6",
        "O7",
        "U3"
      ],
      "text": "Opposing predictions for the fixed pair: stale-pending duplicates yield normal eight versus higher seven recognitions; useful extra bulk coverage instead retains eight in the higher dose. A conforming replay contradicting the predicted counts defeats the harmful-dose account. A feasible bulk-free schedule achieving eight under the unchanged constraints defeats the no-opening conclusion."
    }
  ]
}
